CONTENT.conv.push(
  { id: 'vx021', diff: 3, ctx: 'Listen to a conversation between a student and a professor.', names: { W: 'Professor', M: 'Student' }, lines: [
    ['M', "Professor Nakamura, I've been reading about behavioral economics for my term paper, and I'm kind of torn about my thesis."],
    ['W', "Torn in what way?"],
    ['M', "Well, I wanted to argue that nudges, like automatic enrollment in retirement plans, are a cheap and effective way to change behavior. But then I found a few studies suggesting the effects fade after a couple of years."],
    ['W', "Interesting. And that bothers you because it undermines your argument?"],
    ['M', "Yes, exactly. I don't want to ignore evidence, but I don't want my paper to just say 'it's complicated,' either."],
    ['W', "I'd push back on that last bit. 'It's complicated' isn't a thesis, but a qualified claim can be a very strong one."],
    ['M', "Like saying nudges work well for one-time decisions, but not for habits that need to be repeated?"],
    ['W', "Precisely. Enrolling in a plan happens once; the default does the work afterward. Something like eating healthier requires a choice every day."],
    ['M', "So the distinction might be the real contribution of my paper."],
    ['W', "It could be. I'd just caution you not to overgeneralize from two or three studies. Check whether the contexts are comparable."],
    ['M', "Okay. Should I send you a revised outline once I've sorted that out?"],
    ['W', "Please do. And if you can, find at least one study that contradicts your refined claim. Addressing it will make your argument far more convincing."]],
    qs: [
      { q: 'What is the student\'s main difficulty?', o: ["He is behind on his deadline", "He is unsure how to keep a clear argument given contradictory evidence", "He does not understand behavioral economics", "He cannot find enough sources"], a: 1, x: "Il ne veut ni ignorer les études ni conclure « it's complicated »." },
      { q: 'What does the professor mean when she says, "a qualified claim can be a very strong one"?', o: ["Claims must always be proved by numbers", "Only experts should make claims", "A precise, limited argument can be more convincing than a broad one", "Weak arguments should be removed"], a: 2, x: "Une thèse nuancée et précise peut être plus solide qu'une thèse trop large." },
      { q: 'What does the professor recommend at the end?', o: ["Searching for evidence that challenges his refined claim and addressing it", "Using only older studies", "Interviewing economists", "Dropping the nudge topic"], a: 0, x: "Trouver une étude qui contredit sa thèse affinée et y répondre." }] },
  { id: 'vx022', diff: 3, ctx: 'Listen to a conversation between two students in a finance class.', names: NX, lines: [
    ['W', "Did you finish the valuation assignment? I've been staring at the same spreadsheet for two hours."],
    ['M', "Mostly. What's giving you trouble?"],
    ['W', "The discount rate. I used the company's average borrowing cost, but my result is way higher than the actual stock price."],
    ['M', "Hmm, that's probably the issue. The discount rate shouldn't be the cost of debt alone. You're supposed to use the weighted average cost of capital, so you have to include the cost of equity as well."],
    ['W', "Oh, wait. Of course. Equity holders expect a higher return than lenders because they take more risk."],
    ['M', "Right. Which actually pushes the rate up, so your valuation would go down, closer to the market price."],
    ['W', "Hold on. If it's closer to the market price, does that mean the market is right and my model is wrong?"],
    ['M', "Not necessarily. It just means that, with those inputs, the market is pricing the company the way a standard model would. Professor Kim said a model is only as good as its assumptions."],
    ['W', "Hmm, true. And my growth assumption was pretty optimistic, now that I think about it."],
    ['M', "I assumed five percent, and I'm still not sure that's realistic."],
    ['W', "Maybe we should both run a second version with a lower growth rate and compare."],
    ['M', "Good idea. That would show how sensitive the result is. I think she'd like that."]],
    qs: [
      { q: 'What error did the woman make in her valuation?', o: ["She forgot to include growth", "She used the wrong stock price", "She used only the cost of debt as the discount rate", "She used the cost of equity alone"], a: 2, x: "Elle n'a utilisé que le coût de la dette ; il faut le WACC." },
      { q: 'What does the man imply when he says, "a model is only as good as its assumptions"?', o: ["Models should never be trusted", "Market prices are always correct", "The professor made a mistake", "Results depend heavily on the inputs chosen"], a: 3, x: "La qualité du résultat dépend des hypothèses retenues." },
      { q: 'What do the students plan to do next?', o: ["Ignore the market price", "Use the cost of debt after all", "Ask the professor for a different assignment", "Test a version with a lower growth rate"], a: 3, x: "Une seconde version avec une croissance plus faible, pour mesurer la sensibilité." }] },
  { id: 'vx023', diff: 3, ctx: 'Listen to a conversation between a student and a financial aid advisor.', names: { W: 'Advisor', M: 'Student' }, lines: [
    ['M', "Hi, I received my financial aid package, and I think there's been a mistake. My grant went down by two thousand dollars compared with last year."],
    ['W', "Let me look at your file. Hmm. Your family's reported income went up last year. Do you know why?"],
    ['M', "My dad changed jobs, so he earned more, but only for about six months. Then the company he joined closed, and he's unemployed now."],
    ['W', "I see. That's an important detail, because the form is based on the previous year's income, so it doesn't capture what's happening now."],
    ['M', "So is there anything I can do? It doesn't seem fair."],
    ['W', "There is. It's called a professional judgment review. You submit a letter explaining the change in circumstances, along with proof, such as a termination letter or unemployment documents."],
    ['M', "Does it usually work?"],
    ['W', "I can't promise anything, but when the documentation is clear, aid is often adjusted. The key is to submit before the end of the month."],
    ['M', "That's only ten days away. What if my father can't find the paperwork?"],
    ['W', "Then get whatever you can: pay stubs, a notice from the state office. And explain what's missing. An incomplete application is better than a late one."],
    ['M', "Okay. I'll start tonight."]],
    qs: [
      { q: 'Why was the student\'s grant reduced?', o: ["His grades dropped", "He changed his major", "The university has less money", "The aid calculation used family income that no longer reflects reality"], a: 3, x: "Le calcul repose sur le revenu de l'année précédente, plus élevé." },
      { q: 'What can the student do to try to get more aid?', o: ["Wait until next year", "Request a review with documentation of his father's job loss", "Apply for a loan only", "Transfer to another university"], a: 1, x: "Demande de « professional judgment review » avec justificatifs." },
      { q: 'What does the advisor mean by "An incomplete application is better than a late one"?', o: ["Missing a deadline is more harmful than missing some documents", "The student should not mention his father", "Late applications are accepted without penalty", "Documents are not required"], a: 0, x: "Mieux vaut un dossier incomplet à temps qu'un dossier complet en retard." }] },
  { id: 'vx024', diff: 2, ctx: 'Listen to a conversation between two students working on a group project.', names: NX, lines: [
    ['M', "So, I've got a confession. I haven't started on the literature review."],
    ['W', "Seriously? It's due in four days. I've been waiting for it to write the methodology."],
    ['M', "I know, I know. I was waiting on Jordan. He said he'd send me a list of articles, but I haven't heard anything."],
    ['W', "Okay, but you could have told us. I mean, we divided the work so that nobody would be waiting on anybody."],
    ['M', "You're right. I should have raised it earlier. I thought it'd sort itself out."],
    ['W', "Well, let's sort it out now. Can you search the databases yourself tonight? You only need about ten solid sources."],
    ['M', "I can do that. But honestly, I'm not sure I can write the whole review by Friday."],
    ['W', "What if I start the section on research methods, since I already know what I need there, and you handle the section on earlier findings?"],
    ['M', "That would help a lot. But doesn't that mean you'd be doing more than your share?"],
    ['W', "A bit, yes. But if we fail the project, it's all of us who suffer. We can raise it with Jordan later."],
    ['M', "Fair enough. I'll text him now and tell him I'm moving on without his list."],
    ['W', "Good. And next time, tell us as soon as something's blocking you."]],
    qs: [
      { q: 'What is the woman mainly upset about?', o: ["The man chose the wrong topic", "The professor gave a bad grade", "The man did not tell the group about a delay", "Jordan changed the project deadline"], a: 2, x: "Il n'a pas signalé son retard : « you could have told us »." },
      { q: 'Why does the woman offer to start a section she was not assigned?', o: ["To avoid working with Jordan", "Because she dislikes the man's writing", "To help the group meet the deadline", "Because the professor asked her to"], a: 2, x: "« if we fail the project, it's all of us who suffer »." },
      { q: 'What can be inferred about the woman\'s attitude toward Jordan?', o: ["She thinks he should be removed immediately", "She is willing to discuss his role later", "She does not know him", "She will do his work for him"], a: 1, x: "« We can raise it with Jordan later »." }] },
  { id: 'vx025', diff: 3, ctx: 'Listen to a conversation between a student and an academic advisor.', names: { W: 'Advisor', M: 'Student' }, lines: [
    ['M', "Thanks for meeting me. I'm thinking about switching from engineering to economics, but I'm honestly not sure it's the right move."],
    ['W', "That's a big decision. What's driving it?"],
    ['M', "Well, I like math, but the labs in my engineering classes feel, I don't know, repetitive. Whereas the economics elective I took last spring made me actually curious about how the world works."],
    ['W', "So it's more about interest than difficulty."],
    ['M', "Mostly, yes. Although my GPA in engineering is only a three point one, and I worry that's part of it."],
    ['W', "It's worth being honest about that. But a three point one isn't a disaster. Is it an excuse, or is it a signal?"],
    ['M', "Hmm. I guess it's a signal, because I'm not putting in the effort on subjects I don't care about."],
    ['W', "That's a pretty insightful answer. Let me ask something else. Have you considered a double major, or maybe a minor in economics?"],
    ['M', "I did, but the schedule would be crazy. I'd probably need an extra year."],
    ['W', "True, and the extra year costs money. Before you decide, I'd suggest taking one more upper-level economics course next term, and talking to a few people who work in the field."],
    ['M', "So, test the idea before committing."],
    ['W', "Right. The deadline to change majors isn't until the end of the next semester, so you're not under pressure."]],
    qs: [
      { q: 'What is the main reason the student wants to change majors?', o: ["He is more interested in economics", "He wants to finish faster", "His grades are too low to continue", "His advisor recommended it"], a: 0, x: "Principalement l'intérêt, pas la difficulté." },
      { q: 'What does the advisor mean by "Is it an excuse, or is it a signal?"', o: ["She is asking about his schedule", "She wants to know if his grade reflects a deeper lack of interest", "She believes engineering is too difficult", "She thinks he is lying"], a: 1, x: "Les notes révèlent-elles un manque d'intérêt réel ? Il répond « signal »." },
      { q: 'What does the advisor recommend?', o: ["Stay in engineering", "Try another upper-level economics course and talk to professionals before deciding", "Change majors immediately", "Take a year off"], a: 1, x: "Tester l'idée avant de s'engager ; pas de pression de délai." }] },
  { id: 'vx026', diff: 3, ctx: 'Listen to a conversation between a student and a professor after a lecture on accounting.', names: { W: 'Professor', M: 'Student' }, lines: [
    ['M', "Professor Haddad, do you have a moment? I didn't quite follow the part about revenue recognition."],
    ['W', "Of course. What confused you?"],
    ['M', "Well, if a company receives payment today for a service it will provide next year, why can't it count that money as revenue right away? The cash is already there."],
    ['W', "Good question, and it's one many people ask. Think about it this way. Cash in the bank isn't the same as having earned it."],
    ['M', "Because the company still owes the customer the service?"],
    ['W', "Exactly. Until it delivers, it has an obligation, which goes on the balance sheet as a liability, sometimes called deferred revenue."],
    ['M', "Hmm, so the money is, sort of, borrowed against the future service."],
    ['W', "That's a helpful way to put it, although I wouldn't call it a loan. The principle is that revenue is recorded when it's earned, not when the cash arrives."],
    ['M', "And what happens when a subscription company sells a three-year plan?"],
    ['W', "They'd recognize one-thirty-sixth of the amount each month. That prevents the company from looking more profitable in the first month than it really is."],
    ['M', "I see. So the rule is, in a way, protecting investors from being misled."],
    ['W', "Precisely. Accounting is partly about timing, and the timing has to reflect economic reality."]],
    qs: [
      { q: 'What does the student not understand at the beginning?', o: ["How to read a balance sheet", "Why companies need cash", "What a subscription is", "Why advance payments are not immediately counted as revenue"], a: 3, x: "Pourquoi l'argent déjà reçu n'est pas du revenu immédiat." },
      { q: 'Why does the professor say she would not call deferred revenue "a loan"?', o: ["Because companies never borrow", "To point out that the comparison is helpful but not exactly accurate", "Because it is legally a gift", "Because it is not on the balance sheet"], a: 1, x: "Analogie utile mais imprécise : c'est une obligation de livrer un service." },
      { q: 'According to the professor, what is the purpose of recognizing revenue over time?', o: ["To make financial statements reflect the company's actual performance", "To reduce taxes", "To delay payments to customers", "To increase cash"], a: 0, x: "Éviter que l'entreprise paraisse plus rentable qu'elle ne l'est." }] },
  { id: 'vx027', diff: 2, ctx: 'Listen to a conversation between two students about an internship offer.', names: NX, lines: [
    ['W', "I got an offer from the bank. A summer internship, but it's in a different city."],
    ['M', "That's fantastic! Why do you sound so unenthusiastic?"],
    ['W', "Well, the pay is decent, but I'd have to find an apartment for ten weeks, and in that city, short-term rentals are crazy expensive."],
    ['M', "Does the bank help with housing?"],
    ['W', "They offer a stipend, but it covers maybe half the rent."],
    ['M', "Hmm. And what about the other option you mentioned? The small startup near campus?"],
    ['W', "They haven't made an offer yet. They said they'd decide by next Friday, but the bank wants an answer in three days."],
    ['M', "So you'd have to accept the bank's offer without knowing about the startup."],
    ['W', "Right. And the startup would probably give me more responsibility, but the bank looks better on a résumé, I think."],
    ['M', "Sure, but would you rather have a name on your résumé or real experience?"],
    ['W', "That's the thing. I don't know whether the startup would actually deliver on that."],
    ['M', "Why not ask the bank for a few more days? Companies often allow that if you're polite about it."],
    ['W', "Hmm, I hadn't thought of that. Worst case, they say no, and I'm in the same position I'm in now."]],
    qs: [
      { q: 'Why is the woman hesitant to accept the bank\'s offer?', o: ["The salary is too low", "She did not pass the interview", "Housing costs would be high, and she is uncertain about another option", "She dislikes banking"], a: 2, x: "Logement cher et attente de la réponse de la startup." },
      { q: 'What does the man suggest?', o: ["Rejecting both offers", "Accepting the bank's offer immediately", "Calling the startup every day", "Asking the bank for more time"], a: 3, x: "Demander poliment quelques jours de plus à la banque." },
      { q: 'What does the woman mean when she says, "I don\'t know whether the startup would actually deliver on that"?', o: ["She believes the startup will not hire her", "She is worried about moving", "She is unsure the startup will really give her real responsibility", "She thinks the startup cannot pay her"], a: 2, x: "« deliver on that » : tenir sa promesse de responsabilités." }] },
  { id: 'vx028', diff: 3, ctx: 'Listen to a conversation between a student and a librarian about a research project.', names: { W: 'Librarian', M: 'Student' }, lines: [
    ['M', "Hi, I'm working on a paper about the economic effects of remote work, and I'm having trouble finding good sources."],
    ['W', "Sure, I can help. What have you tried so far?"],
    ['M', "I mostly searched online and found a lot of articles, but most of them seem to be opinion pieces, or reports from companies that sell remote-work software."],
    ['W', "That's a good observation. You're right to be cautious, because those sources may have an interest in showing certain results."],
    ['M', "Right. My professor wants peer-reviewed articles, preferably from the last five years."],
    ['W', "Then let's use the economics database. Try searching for 'telework' as well as 'remote work.' Researchers often use different terms."],
    ['M', "Oh, I wouldn't have thought of that. It's the same thing, isn't it?"],
    ['W', "Basically, yes, but the vocabulary varies by field. Also, once you find one useful article, look at its reference list. That's a shortcut to related research."],
    ['M', "Like, following the trail backward?"],
    ['W', "Exactly. And you can also check who has cited it since, which gets you the newest work."],
    ['M', "That's really useful. One more thing. Some of the best articles seem to be behind paywalls."],
    ['W', "Don't worry about that. If we subscribe, you can access them with your student login, and if not, we can request a copy for you within a couple of days."]],
    qs: [
      { q: 'What is the student\'s problem?', o: ["He needs more time for the assignment", "He is finding sources that are not suitable for academic research", "He does not know the topic", "He cannot access the library website"], a: 1, x: "Articles d'opinion et rapports d'entreprises intéressées." },
      { q: 'Why does the librarian suggest searching for "telework"?', o: ["It will reduce the number of results", "It is the professor's favorite term", "Different researchers use different terms for the same idea", "It is a more recent concept"], a: 2, x: "Le vocabulaire varie selon les domaines." },
      { q: 'What does the librarian mean by saying that company reports "may have an interest in showing certain results"?', o: ["They are too technical", "They are out of date", "They are always false", "They might be biased"], a: 3, x: "Intérêt commercial : possible biais." }] }
);

/* ---------- Academic talks ---------- */
CONTENT.talk.push(
  { id: 'tx001', diff: 2, g: 'W', ctx: 'Listen to part of a lecture in a biology class.', text: "Today I want to look at how honeybees communicate, because it's one of the most remarkable examples of information sharing in the animal kingdom. When a forager bee discovers a rich patch of flowers, she returns to the hive and performs what scientists call the waggle dance. This takes place on the vertical surface of the honeycomb, inside a dark hive, so her nestmates mostly follow the dance by touch rather than by sight. She walks in a straight line while shaking her body from side to side, then loops back to her starting point and repeats the pattern. Now, what makes the dance so impressive is how precise it is. The angle of the straight run, measured against the vertical, corresponds to the direction of the food relative to the sun. And the duration of the waggle indicates the distance: the longer the waggle, the farther away the flowers are. This code was deciphered by the Austrian zoologist Karl von Frisch, who shared a Nobel Prize in 1973 for this work. He set out dishes of sugar water at different distances from a hive and recorded how the returning bees danced. What I find striking is that the dance is a kind of symbolic language. It doesn't show the food itself. It represents a location, and the other bees have to interpret the message and turn it into a flight path. Interestingly, the system isn't flawless. Scouts sometimes disagree about which patch is best, and the colony has to settle the matter somehow. We'll come back to that next week, because bees actually have a way of voting.",
    qs: [
      { q: 'What is the lecture mainly about?', o: ["How bees produce honey", "Why bees live in hives", "How scientists breed bees", "How bees share information about food sources"], a: 3, x: "La danse frétillante transmet la direction et la distance de la nourriture." },
      { q: 'What does the length of the waggle indicate?', o: ["The distance to the food", "The quality of the flowers", "The number of bees needed", "The time of day"], a: 0, x: "« the longer the waggle, the farther away the flowers are »." },
      { q: 'How did Karl von Frisch learn what the dance meant?', o: ["He photographed the hive at night", "He placed sugar water at various distances and observed the dances", "He removed the bees' wings", "He compared the bees with other insects"], a: 1, x: "Coupelles d'eau sucrée placées à différentes distances du rucher." },
      { q: 'Why does the professor call the dance "a kind of symbolic language"?', o: ["The bees dance only in large groups", "The bees can make sounds like speech", "The dance represents the location instead of showing it directly", "The dance happens in the dark"], a: 2, x: "Un code que les autres abeilles doivent interpréter, pas une démonstration directe." },
      { q: 'What will the professor probably discuss in the next class?', o: ["How bees choose between competing food sources", "How bees build honeycomb", "Why bees dance in the dark", "How von Frisch won his prize"], a: 0, x: "Elle annonce que les abeilles ont « a way of voting » quand les éclaireuses ne sont pas d'accord." }] },
  { id: 'tx002', diff: 2, g: 'M', ctx: 'Listen to part of a lecture in an economics class.', text: "Let's talk about two ideas that sound similar but lead to very different decisions: opportunity cost and sunk cost. Opportunity cost is the value of the next best alternative you give up when you make a choice. Suppose you spend a Saturday working a shift that pays eighty dollars. The opportunity cost of an afternoon at the beach is not zero, even though the beach is free. It's the eighty dollars you could have earned. Economists insist that every choice has this hidden price. It also explains why a student who skips a lecture to sleep in hasn't really saved anything: she has simply traded the lecture for the sleep, and she should ask whether that was the better deal. Now, a sunk cost is the opposite kind of idea. It's money or effort you've already spent and can't recover. Imagine you've paid for a movie ticket, and twenty minutes in, you realize the film is terrible. Many people stay, because they paid. But the ticket money is gone whether you leave or not. The only question that matters is whether the remaining time is better spent watching the movie or doing something else. Rationally, sunk costs should be ignored. In practice, though, people have a hard time doing this. Companies keep funding failing projects because they've already invested millions. Governments continue to build unpopular infrastructure for the same reason. This is sometimes called the sunk cost fallacy. So here's a good rule of thumb. When you decide, look forward, not backward. Ask what you'll gain and what you'll give up from this point on. For instance, if you've already spent three hours on a hopeless assignment, those hours are gone. The sensible question is whether another hour would improve the result enough to justify the time.",
    qs: [
      { q: 'What is the main purpose of the lecture?', o: ["To explain how movie tickets are priced", "To describe how companies make profit", "To distinguish two concepts that affect decision making", "To argue that free activities have no cost"], a: 2, x: "Coût d'opportunité vs coûts irrécupérables." },
      { q: 'In the professor\'s example, what is the opportunity cost of going to the beach?', o: ["The eighty dollars not earned at work", "The cost of transportation", "The price of a movie ticket", "Nothing, because the beach is free"], a: 0, x: "Le gain sacrifié : 80 $." },
      { q: 'Why does the professor mention the movie ticket?', o: ["To show how to save money", "To illustrate money that has already been spent and cannot be recovered", "To explain opportunity cost", "To criticize the film industry"], a: 1, x: "Exemple de coût irrécupérable." },
      { q: 'What does the professor suggest people should do when making decisions?', o: ["Think about past investments first", "Follow what others do", "Avoid all risk", "Focus on future gains and losses"], a: 3, x: "« look forward, not backward »." },
      { q: 'What can be inferred about organizations that keep funding failing projects?', o: ["They have no competitors", "They have unlimited money", "They follow economic theory closely", "They are influenced by the sunk cost fallacy"], a: 3, x: "Ils continuent à cause de ce qu'ils ont déjà investi." }] },
  { id: 'tx003', diff: 3, g: 'W', ctx: 'Listen to part of a lecture in a behavioral science class.', text: "Traditional economics assumes that people value things according to their usefulness, regardless of whether they own them. But behavioral research suggests otherwise. One of the best-known findings is the endowment effect. In a classic experiment, researchers gave half of the students in a room a coffee mug, and the other half nothing. Then they asked the mug owners how much they'd accept to sell it, and the others how much they'd pay to buy it. Standard theory predicts that the two numbers should be roughly equal. In reality, the owners demanded about twice as much as the buyers were willing to pay. Why? One explanation is loss aversion, a concept developed by Daniel Kahneman and Amos Tversky. They argued that losses loom larger than equivalent gains. Giving up the mug feels like a loss, which hurts more than the pleasure of gaining an identical mug. Notice that the students had no special attachment to the mugs; they had owned them for only a few minutes. That is what makes the result so striking. Now, I should add a caveat. Some later studies have found that the effect shrinks when people are experienced traders, or when they don't think of the item as something to keep. So it's not a universal law. It may depend on attachment and context. Still, the implications are far-reaching. It helps explain why people hold on to losing investments, why free trial periods increase sales, and why it's so difficult to persuade someone to give up an old habit once it feels like theirs. So the next time you find it hard to throw something away, ask yourself whether you'd buy it today at the price you're asking.",
    qs: [
      { q: 'What is the main idea of the lecture?', o: ["Students are bad at negotiating", "Mugs are more valuable than money", "Economists have never studied human behavior", "People's valuations of objects can change once they own them"], a: 3, x: "L'effet de dotation : posséder augmente la valeur perçue." },
      { q: 'In the experiment, how did the mug owners differ from the potential buyers?', o: ["Both groups gave identical values", "Buyers offered more than owners requested", "Owners wanted about twice as much money as buyers were prepared to pay", "Owners were willing to give the mug for free"], a: 2, x: "Les propriétaires exigeaient environ le double." },
      { q: 'How does loss aversion explain the result?', o: ["Losing something feels worse than gaining something of equal value feels good", "Owners forget the price they paid", "People dislike coffee", "Buyers always lie about prices"], a: 0, x: "Les pertes pèsent plus que les gains équivalents." },
      { q: 'Why does the professor mention experienced traders?', o: ["To explain how mugs are sold", "To say traders never lose money", "To prove the experiment was faked", "To show that the effect is not always as strong"], a: 3, x: "L'effet diminue pour les traders expérimentés : nuance." },
      { q: 'What does the professor imply about the endowment effect?', o: ["It is real but depends on circumstances", "It is a universal law of human behavior", "It applies only to students", "It has been completely disproven"], a: 0, x: "« not a universal law. It may depend on attachment and context »." }] },
  { id: 'tx004', diff: 2, g: 'M', ctx: 'Listen to part of a talk in a personal finance class.', text: "Today we'll look at compound interest, which Albert Einstein is often said to have called the eighth wonder of the world. He almost certainly never said it, but the idea is powerful enough to deserve the compliment. Let's begin with simple interest. If you deposit a thousand dollars at five percent simple interest, you earn fifty dollars every year, always calculated on the original sum. After ten years, you'd have fifteen hundred dollars. Compound interest works differently. Each year, the interest is added to your balance, so the following year you earn interest on a larger amount. In year one you earn fifty dollars. In year two you earn five percent of one thousand and fifty dollars, which is fifty-two dollars and fifty cents. That difference looks trivial at first. But the gap widens steadily. After ten years, the same deposit would be worth about sixteen hundred and twenty-nine dollars. After thirty years, it would exceed four thousand dollars, while simple interest would give you only twenty-five hundred. The key ingredient is time. Because the growth builds on itself, the later years contribute far more than the early ones. This is why financial advisors say that the best moment to start saving is as soon as possible, even if the sums are small. Someone who starts early with modest amounts can end up well ahead of someone who starts a decade later and has to contribute considerably more each month just to catch up. Of course, compounding works in the opposite direction on debt. A credit card balance that is left unpaid grows in exactly the same way, which is why high-interest borrowing can be so difficult to escape.",
    qs: [
      { q: 'What is the main topic of the talk?', o: ["How banks decide whom to lend money to", "Albert Einstein's contribution to economics", "How to choose a bank account", "How interest grows over time when it is added to the balance"], a: 3, x: "Les intérêts composés et leur croissance dans le temps." },
      { q: 'What does the speaker imply about the Einstein quotation?', o: ["Einstein probably never said it", "Einstein said it in a famous lecture", "It was written by an economist", "It is a good definition of simple interest"], a: 0, x: "« He almost certainly never said it, but the idea is powerful »." },
      { q: 'How does compound interest differ from simple interest?', o: ["It is always calculated at a higher rate", "Interest is calculated on a balance that includes earlier interest", "Interest is calculated on the original amount only", "It has to be paid out every month"], a: 1, x: "Les intérêts s'ajoutent au capital et produisent à leur tour des intérêts." },
      { q: 'What advice does the speaker give?', o: ["Wait until you can save a large amount", "Invest only in stocks", "Start saving as early as possible, even with small sums", "Avoid banks altogether"], a: 2, x: "Commencer tôt, même avec de petites sommes : le temps est le facteur clé." },
      { q: 'Why does the speaker mention credit card balances?', o: ["To show that credit cards offer the best interest rates", "To show that compounding can also work against borrowers", "To explain why simple interest is safer", "To argue that people should not save"], a: 1, x: "Une dette impayée croît de la même manière : la capitalisation joue aussi contre l'emprunteur." }] },
  { id: 'tx005', diff: 2, g: 'W', ctx: 'Listen to part of a lecture in an accounting class.', text: "Let's talk about depreciation, a concept that confuses many beginners. Imagine a delivery company buys a truck for sixty thousand dollars. The truck will be useful for about five years. Should the company record sixty thousand dollars of expense in the year it buys the truck? Accounting says no. The principle is called matching: expenses should be recorded in the same period as the revenues they help produce. Since the truck helps generate revenue for five years, its cost should be spread over those five years. That spreading is depreciation. The simplest approach is the straight-line method. You subtract the truck's expected resale value, say ten thousand dollars, from the purchase price, and divide the result by five. That gives ten thousand dollars of depreciation expense per year. In other words, the company spreads the net cost of the truck evenly across its five years of service. Notice something important: depreciation isn't a cash payment. The cash left the company when the truck was bought. Each year, depreciation simply reduces the truck's recorded value, which accountants call its book value, and it also reduces reported profit. Another method, called accelerated depreciation, records more expense in the early years, since a vehicle often loses value faster when it's new. Companies choose the method that best reflects how the asset is used, and sometimes tax rules influence the decision. When the truck is eventually sold, any difference between its book value and the sale price is recorded as a gain or a loss. But the main idea is the same. Depreciation is about allocating a cost over time, not about paying for something again.",
    qs: [
      { q: 'What is the lecture mainly about?', o: ["How to calculate taxes", "How to buy vehicles", "How companies pay employees", "Why and how the cost of an asset is spread over its useful life"], a: 3, x: "Le principe d'amortissement." },
      { q: 'According to the professor, why isn\'t the full cost of the truck recorded as an expense in the first year?', o: ["Expenses should be matched with the revenues the asset helps to produce", "The truck is not worth anything", "The company has no cash", "The truck must be sold first"], a: 0, x: "Principe de rattachement (« matching »)." },
      { q: 'Using the straight-line method, how much depreciation expense is recorded each year in the example?', o: ["Twelve thousand dollars", "Sixty thousand dollars", "Ten thousand dollars", "Six thousand dollars"], a: 2, x: "(60 000 - 10 000) / 5 = 10 000." },
      { q: 'What does the professor say about depreciation and cash?', o: ["Depreciation does not involve a cash payment", "Depreciation is paid to the government", "Depreciation increases cash", "Depreciation is a yearly cash payment"], a: 0, x: "« depreciation isn't a cash payment »." },
      { q: 'Why might a company use accelerated depreciation?', o: ["It reduces the purchase price", "Assets such as vehicles lose more value early in their lives", "It increases resale value", "It is required for all companies"], a: 1, x: "Un véhicule perd plus de valeur au début." }] },
  { id: 'tx006', diff: 3, g: 'M', ctx: 'Listen to part of a lecture in a finance class.', text: "Investors are often told not to put all their eggs in one basket. That advice, diversification, is one of the few ideas in finance that economists almost universally accept. But the interesting question is why it works. The answer lies in correlation. Suppose you own shares in one company, and its stock falls fifty percent. You lose a huge amount. If instead you own shares in twenty companies, a bad outcome for one has only a limited effect on your total. That's the basic logic: the more separate bets you hold, the less any single failure can hurt you. However, diversification doesn't eliminate all risk. Economists distinguish between two types. Company-specific risk, like a scandal or a failed product, can be reduced by holding many stocks. But market risk, the risk that the whole economy goes into recession, affects nearly everything, so you can't diversify it away within the stock market. Now, here's a subtlety. The benefit of diversification depends on how closely your investments move together. Owning ten technology companies is less protective than owning shares in a technology firm, a utility, a healthcare company, and some government bonds, because the latter group reacts differently to economic shocks. The problem is that in a severe crisis, correlations tend to rise. In 2008, assets that normally behaved independently all fell at once, and many investors discovered that their portfolios were less diversified than they thought. So the lesson isn't that diversification is useless. It's that it offers less protection precisely when you need it most. In other words, the safety net is weakest at the very moment you lean on it.",
    qs: [
      { q: 'What is the main point of the lecture?', o: ["Diversification reduces some risks but has limits", "Technology stocks are the safest", "Government bonds always lose value", "Investors should avoid all stocks"], a: 0, x: "La diversification réduit une partie du risque, pas tout." },
      { q: 'What does the professor mean by "company-specific risk"?', o: ["The risk of buying bonds", "The cost of trading", "The danger that the whole economy declines", "Problems that affect only one firm"], a: 3, x: "Scandale, produit raté : propre à une entreprise." },
      { q: 'Why does the professor say that ten technology companies are less protective than a mixed portfolio?', o: ["Mixed portfolios have no risk", "Utilities pay higher dividends", "Technology companies are unprofitable", "Similar companies tend to react similarly to the same events"], a: 3, x: "Corrélation élevée entre actifs similaires." },
      { q: 'What happened in 2008, according to the professor?', o: ["All investors became rich", "Normally independent assets declined together", "Bond prices rose sharply", "Diversification worked perfectly"], a: 1, x: "Les corrélations ont augmenté en crise." },
      { q: 'What does the professor imply in the final sentence?', o: ["Its protection tends to weaken during major crises", "Investors should expect it to fail only in rare cases that no one can predict", "Only experts can diversify", "Diversification is worthless"], a: 0, x: "Moins de protection « precisely when you need it most »." }] },
  { id: 'tx007', diff: 2, g: 'W', ctx: 'Listen to part of a lecture in a marine biology class.', text: "Coral reefs cover less than one percent of the ocean floor, but they support roughly a quarter of all marine species. That's why scientists are so concerned about coral bleaching. To understand it, we need to know that corals live in partnership with tiny algae, called zooxanthellae, that live inside their tissue. The algae use sunlight to produce sugars, and the coral uses these sugars as food. In return, the coral gives the algae shelter and nutrients. The algae also give corals their bright colors. When the water gets too warm, even by one or two degrees above normal for several weeks, this partnership breaks down. The algae start producing chemicals that are harmful, so the coral expels them. Without the algae, the coral turns white, which is what we call bleaching. Now, here's an important point. Bleached coral isn't dead. It's starving. If the temperature returns to normal quickly enough, algae can move back in and the coral can recover. But if the heat lasts too long, the coral dies and the reef structure slowly erodes. When that happens, the fish, crabs, and other animals that shelter in the reef lose their habitat, and coastal communities lose a natural barrier against storm waves. Some reefs have shown surprising resilience. In certain regions, corals have adapted by hosting heat-tolerant algae. Marine biologists have also noticed that reefs that have already lived through warm spells tend to bleach less severely than reefs that rarely experience heat. Researchers are now exploring whether we could help this process along. That's controversial, though, because it could distract from the larger problem of reducing carbon emissions, which is what drives ocean warming in the first place.",
    qs: [
      { q: 'What is the main topic of the lecture?', o: ["Why corals lose their color and what it means", "How algae are harvested", "The history of coral reefs", "How fish use reefs"], a: 0, x: "Le blanchissement du corail." },
      { q: 'What do corals receive from algae?', o: ["Protection from predators", "Heat", "Sugars used as food", "Salt"], a: 2, x: "Les algues produisent des sucres." },
      { q: 'According to the professor, what happens to bleached coral?', o: ["It changes species", "It is already dead", "It is starving but may recover if conditions improve", "It grows faster"], a: 2, x: "« Bleached coral isn't dead. It's starving »." },
      { q: 'Why does the professor mention heat-tolerant algae?', o: ["To argue that bleaching is harmless", "To describe how algae are expelled", "To explain why reefs are colorful", "To show that some corals can adapt to warmer water"], a: 3, x: "Certains coraux s'adaptent en accueillant des algues tolérantes." },
      { q: 'Why is assisted adaptation controversial?', o: ["It is too expensive to test", "It might draw attention away from reducing emissions", "It would harm the algae", "It has failed in every experiment"], a: 1, x: "Risque de détourner de la réduction des émissions." }] },
  { id: 'tx008', diff: 3, g: 'M', ctx: 'Listen to part of a lecture in a literature class.', text: "For a long time, readers of Homer's Iliad and Odyssey assumed that the poems were composed in writing by a single genius, much as a modern novelist would do. In the 1920s, a young scholar named Milman Parry challenged this assumption. He noticed that Homer repeats certain phrases constantly: 'swift-footed Achilles,' 'rosy-fingered dawn,' 'wine-dark sea.' To a modern reader, this can seem repetitive or even lazy. Parry's insight was that these formulas weren't a weakness. They were tools. A poet performing live, without a written text, needed ready-made units that fit the rhythm of the verse. If the line needed a certain number of syllables to describe Achilles, the poet had a phrase that fit exactly. To test his theory, Parry traveled to the Balkans, where he recorded illiterate singers who performed epic songs that lasted for hours. They, too, relied on formulas, and no two performances were identical. This suggested that the Homeric poems emerged from a long oral tradition, where each performer recomposed the story slightly as he sang. In other words, there was no single definitive text; each performance was a fresh version built from the same traditional materials. Now, scholars still disagree about how the poems were eventually written down, and whether Homer was one poet or several. But Parry changed the question. We stopped asking whether the poems were original in the modern sense, and started asking how a tradition could preserve and transform stories across generations without writing at all. It's a good reminder that the idea of the lone author is only one way of thinking about great literature.",
    qs: [
      { q: 'What is the main point of the lecture?', o: ["The Balkans were home to Homer", "Homer's poems are repetitive and poorly written", "Studying oral performance changed how scholars understand Homer's poems", "Illiterate people cannot compose poetry"], a: 2, x: "Parry a transformé la compréhension de la composition orale." },
      { q: 'According to the lecture, what is the function of formulaic phrases?', o: ["They help the poet compose and perform in a fixed rhythm", "They are used to teach children", "They help readers memorize a text", "They show the poet's lack of talent"], a: 0, x: "Unités toutes faites adaptées au rythme du vers." },
      { q: 'Why did Parry travel to the Balkans?', o: ["To teach singers to write", "To observe living oral singers to test his theory", "To find original manuscripts of Homer", "To study ancient pottery"], a: 1, x: "Enregistrer des chanteurs illettrés." },
      { q: 'What does the professor imply by saying that Parry "changed the question"?', o: ["Parry proved Homer never existed", "The focus moved from individual originality to how oral tradition works", "The poems were declared unimportant", "Scholars stopped studying Homer"], a: 1, x: "On passe de « original ? » à « comment la tradition transmet »." },
      { q: 'What do scholars still debate?', o: ["Whether the poems were recorded in the 1920s", "Whether formulas exist in the poems", "Whether Parry visited the Balkans", "Whether the poems were composed by one poet or several"], a: 3, x: "« whether Homer was one poet or several »." }] },
  { id: 'tx009', diff: 2, g: 'W', ctx: 'Listen to part of a lecture in a behavioral science class.', text: "Imagine you're buying a jacket, and the first one you see is marked at five hundred dollars. Then you find another that costs two hundred and fifty. Suddenly the second jacket seems like a bargain, even though two hundred and fifty dollars is a lot of money. Nothing about the second jacket has changed; only the reference point has. This is an example of anchoring, the tendency to rely too heavily on the first piece of information we encounter. In a famous experiment, Kahneman and Tversky asked people to spin a wheel numbered from one to a hundred, which was secretly rigged to stop at either ten or sixty-five. Then they asked participants to estimate the percentage of African countries in the United Nations. The people who saw sixty-five gave much higher estimates than those who saw ten, even though everyone knew that the wheel was random. The effect is surprisingly robust, and it persists even when people are rewarded for being accurate. Anchoring affects real-world decisions, too. In negotiations, the person who makes the first offer usually pulls the final price toward their number. Sellers often list a high original price so that a discount appears generous. Interestingly, experts are also vulnerable. Studies have found that real estate agents' valuations were influenced by the asking price of a house, although they insisted that they ignored it. So what can we do? One practical strategy is to deliberately consider reasons why the anchor might be wrong before forming an estimate. Another approach is to set your own number before you see anyone else's, so that you start from your research rather than from theirs.",
    qs: [
      { q: 'What is anchoring?', o: ["Making decisions in groups", "Buying only discounted items", "Ignoring all numbers", "Giving too much weight to the first information we receive"], a: 3, x: "Le biais d'ancrage." },
      { q: 'Why does the professor mention the spinning wheel experiment?', o: ["To demonstrate that experts are always right", "To show that even clearly random numbers can influence estimates", "To explain how the UN was founded", "To prove that people are good at probability"], a: 1, x: "Un nombre manifestement aléatoire influence les estimations." },
      { q: 'How can sellers use anchoring?', o: ["By selling only to experts", "By avoiding discounts", "By listing a high original price so that the sale price looks attractive", "By hiding the price"], a: 2, x: "Prix initial élevé = remise perçue comme généreuse." },
      { q: 'What did the study of real estate agents show?', o: ["Agents ignore asking prices", "Agents prefer low prices", "Agents rarely estimate prices", "Even professionals were influenced by the asking price despite denying it"], a: 3, x: "Les experts aussi sont vulnérables." },
      { q: 'What strategy does the professor recommend?', o: ["Avoid negotiations", "Ask experts for numbers", "Consider why the anchor might be wrong before estimating", "Accept the first number"], a: 2, x: "Chercher des raisons pour lesquelles l'ancre serait fausse." }] },
  { id: 'tx010', diff: 2, g: 'M', ctx: 'Listen to part of a talk in a psychology class.', text: "Today's topic is something we all do but rarely stop to think about: sleep. Why do we sleep at all? Scientists don't have one complete answer, but several explanations appear to work together. First, sleep allows the body to recover. During deep sleep, tissues are repaired and the immune system is strengthened, which is one reason why people who sleep badly tend to catch more colds. Second, sleep is essential for the brain. While we're asleep, the brain sorts through the information we've taken in during the day and consolidates the important parts into long-term memory. In several studies, students who slept after learning new material remembered considerably more than students who stayed awake for the same period. Third, sleep seems to help the brain clear out waste. Researchers have identified a kind of cleaning system that becomes far more active at night, flushing out substances that accumulate while we're awake. Most adults need between seven and nine hours of sleep, and teenagers need slightly more. Unfortunately, many students get far less than that, particularly around exams. They assume that pulling an all-nighter will give them an edge, but the evidence points the other way. Sleep deprivation makes it harder to concentrate, reduces the ability to solve problems, and affects mood, so a good part of those extra hours of study is wasted. If you want to improve your sleep, a few simple habits can make a real difference. Go to bed at roughly the same time every night, avoid screens for an hour beforehand, and keep your bedroom dark and cool. None of this is complicated, but it does require some discipline.",
    qs: [
      { q: 'What is the talk mainly about?', o: ["How to design a bedroom", "Why teenagers need more rest than adults", "How to study at night", "The reasons we sleep and the benefits of sleep"], a: 3, x: "Les fonctions du sommeil et ses bénéfices." },
      { q: 'According to the speaker, what happens in the brain during sleep?', o: ["Important information is consolidated into long-term memory", "Students forget most of what they learned", "The brain temporarily stops working", "The brain grows larger"], a: 0, x: "Le cerveau trie les informations et fixe les plus importantes en mémoire à long terme." },
      { q: 'What does the speaker say about students who stay up all night before an exam?', o: ["They remember details better than other students", "They suffer only if they are teenagers", "A good part of their extra study time is wasted", "They perform better in the afternoon"], a: 2, x: "Le manque de sommeil nuit à la concentration et à la résolution de problèmes : « the evidence points the other way »." },
      { q: 'Why does the speaker mention students who slept after learning new material?', o: ["To show that sleep supports memory", "To prove that students sleep too little", "To explain how the immune system works", "To argue that teenagers should study at night"], a: 0, x: "Ces étudiants retenaient davantage : le sommeil favorise la mémoire." },
      { q: 'Which habit does the speaker recommend?', o: ["Sleeping in a brightly lit room", "Having coffee in the evening", "Going to bed at about the same time every night", "Checking your phone before sleeping"], a: 2, x: "Heure de coucher régulière, pas d'écrans, chambre sombre et fraîche." }] },
  { id: 'tx011', diff: 3, g: 'W', ctx: 'Listen to part of a lecture in an accounting class.', text: "When we talk about auditing, we're really talking about trust. Investors rely on a company's financial statements, but the managers who prepare them have an incentive to make the results look good. So companies hire external auditors, independent professionals, to examine the statements and give an opinion on whether they fairly represent the company's financial position. Notice the word 'fairly.' An audit doesn't guarantee that the statements are perfect or that no fraud exists. It provides what's called reasonable assurance. Auditors can't check every transaction, so they test samples and focus on areas where mistakes are most likely. A key concept in auditing is professional skepticism. This means auditors shouldn't assume that management is dishonest, but they shouldn't automatically assume it's honest either. They question explanations and look for evidence from outside the company, such as bank confirmations. Now, there's a structural problem here. The company being audited is the one that pays the auditor. This creates a potential conflict of interest. An auditor who annoys the client might lose future business. After the collapse of Enron in 2001, which involved a very well-known auditor, regulators introduced rules limiting the consulting services that auditors can sell to their audit clients, and required lead partners to rotate. Whether these reforms have fully solved the problem is still an open question, but they show that trust in auditing depends on the audit's independence as much as on its technical quality. That's also why many people argue that an audit's value lies less in catching every error than in discouraging managers from taking the risk of misreporting in the first place.",
    qs: [
      { q: 'What is the main idea of the lecture?', o: ["Enron was the only fraud in history", "Auditors must verify every transaction", "Managers never have incentives to mislead", "Audits help create trust in financial statements, but their independence is a concern"], a: 3, x: "Rôle de l'audit et problème d'indépendance." },
      { q: 'What does "reasonable assurance" mean?', o: ["A high level of confidence, though not complete certainty", "A legal promise to investors", "A guarantee that there is no fraud", "An opinion from the company's managers"], a: 0, x: "Ni garantie parfaite, ni absence de contrôle." },
      { q: 'What does professional skepticism require of auditors?', o: ["To assume managers are lying", "To avoid contact with the client", "To trust everything management says", "To question explanations and seek independent evidence"], a: 3, x: "Ni confiance aveugle, ni suspicion systématique." },
      { q: 'Why does the professor say the fact that the company pays the auditor is a problem?', o: ["It reduces the number of audits", "It means auditors work for the government", "It makes audits too expensive", "It may discourage auditors from displeasing the client"], a: 3, x: "Conflit d'intérêts potentiel." },
      { q: 'What can be inferred about the post-Enron reforms?', o: ["They completely eliminated problems", "They banned audits", "They tried to strengthen auditors' independence", "They were designed to help managers"], a: 2, x: "Limiter le conseil et faire tourner les associés." }] },
  { id: 'tx012', diff: 2, g: 'M', ctx: 'Listen to part of a lecture in an economics class.', text: "Let's discuss public goods. A public good has two features. First, it's non-excludable, meaning it's impossible, or very costly, to prevent anyone from using it. Second, it's non-rivalrous, meaning one person's use doesn't reduce the amount available to others. Think of a radio broadcast: once the signal is on the air, one more listener tuning in doesn't reduce what anyone else receives. National defense is a classic example. If a country is protected from invasion, every resident is protected, whether they've paid taxes or not, and protecting one more person costs almost nothing. Compare that with a sandwich. If I eat it, you can't. And the seller can easily refuse to give it to me if I don't pay. Private markets work well for sandwiches because people pay for what they consume. But with public goods, there's a problem called free riding. Since I can enjoy the benefit whether or not I contribute, I have an incentive to let others pay. If everyone thinks this way, nobody pays, and the good is under-provided or not provided at all. It's rather like a group dinner where everybody expects someone else to cover the tip. A good can be extremely valuable to society and still be something a market, left alone, won't supply in sufficient quantity. That's why governments generally fund public goods through taxation. Lighthouses are a famous example, although economists have debated whether private companies did, in fact, provide lighthouse services in the past through port fees. Today, a modern version of the issue appears with open-source software. Thousands of companies use it for free, while only a small number of developers do the work of maintaining it.",
    qs: [
      { q: 'What is the lecture mainly about?', o: ["How sandwiches are priced", "The history of national defense", "The characteristics of public goods and the problem of paying for them", "The role of taxes in private markets"], a: 2, x: "Biens publics et passager clandestin." },
      { q: 'Why does the professor mention a sandwich?', o: ["To criticize private businesses", "To show a good that is rivalrous and excludable", "To describe a public good", "To explain taxation"], a: 1, x: "Contre-exemple : bien privé." },
      { q: 'What is free riding?', o: ["Selling goods below cost", "Traveling without paying", "Using a public good without contributing to its cost", "Providing goods for free"], a: 2, x: "Profiter sans contribuer." },
      { q: 'Why do governments usually fund public goods?', o: ["Governments are more efficient than companies", "The public cannot use them otherwise", "Taxes are lower than prices", "Private markets may not provide enough of them"], a: 3, x: "Sous-production si chacun attend que d'autres paient." },
      { q: 'What is the purpose of the open-source software example?', o: ["To show that the problem of free riding exists today", "To prove that taxes are unnecessary", "To criticize software developers", "To explain how programs are written"], a: 0, x: "Exemple contemporain." }] },
  { id: 'tx013', diff: 2, g: 'W', ctx: 'Listen to part of a lecture in a world history class.', text: "When we think of the Silk Road, we often picture a single road with camel caravans carrying silk from China to Rome. But that image is misleading in several ways. First, it wasn't one road. It was a network of overland and sea routes connecting East Asia, Central Asia, South Asia, the Middle East, and Europe, and the name itself was only invented by a German geographer in the nineteenth century. Second, most traders didn't travel the whole distance. Goods passed through many hands, with merchants buying at one city and selling at the next. A piece of silk might change owners a dozen times before it reached the Mediterranean, becoming more expensive at every stage. The people at the two ends of the chain often never met, and many of them had only a vague idea of where the goods had come from. Third, silk was only one of many products. Spices, glass, horses, paper, and precious metals all traveled along these routes, and so did food crops, such as grapes and peaches. But perhaps the most important cargo wasn't a product at all. It was ideas. Buddhism spread from India into China along these routes. So did technologies such as papermaking, which reached the Islamic world in the eighth century and eventually Europe. Unfortunately, diseases also spread this way, and many historians believe the plague reached Europe through trade networks in the fourteenth century. So, if we want to understand the Silk Road, we should see it less as a trade route and more as a long conversation between civilizations.",
    qs: [
      { q: 'What is the main purpose of the lecture?', o: ["To correct a common picture of the Silk Road", "To describe how silk is made", "To explain Roman trade laws", "To argue that the Silk Road never existed"], a: 0, x: "Elle corrige l'image simpliste." },
      { q: 'Why does the professor mention that goods changed hands many times?', o: ["To describe how routes were built", "To explain why silk became cheaper", "To show that few merchants traveled the entire route", "To criticize merchants"], a: 2, x: "Peu de marchands faisaient tout le trajet." },
      { q: 'According to the professor, what moved along the Silk Road besides goods?', o: ["Ideas, religions, technologies and diseases", "Only armies", "Only money", "Only languages"], a: 0, x: "Idées, bouddhisme, papier, maladies." },
      { q: 'What does the professor mean by calling the Silk Road "a long conversation between civilizations"?', o: ["Merchants spoke many languages", "It emphasizes exchange of ideas and cultures rather than only commerce", "Civilizations disagreed with each other", "The route was very long"], a: 1, x: "Métaphore : échanges culturels." },
      { q: 'What can be inferred about the name "Silk Road"?', o: ["It was used by ancient merchants", "It was the official Chinese name", "It was invented by a Roman", "It was created long after the routes were used"], a: 3, x: "Terme inventé au XIXe siècle." }] },
  { id: 'tx014', diff: 2, g: 'W', ctx: 'Listen to part of a talk in a health science class.', text: "How do vaccines work? To answer that, we first need to look at how the body deals with an infection. When a virus or bacterium gets into your body, the immune system recognizes it as foreign and produces proteins called antibodies to neutralize it. That process takes time, which is why you can feel ill for several days. But there's a silver lining. Once the infection is over, some of the immune cells stay in your body as memory cells. If the same germ ever returns, these cells recognize it quickly and mount a much faster response, often before you notice any symptoms. A vaccine is designed to give you that memory without making you ill. It might contain a harmless fragment of the germ, a weakened version of it, or instructions that tell your own cells to produce one harmless fragment. Your immune system reacts as if the threat were real and builds up its memory cells. Later, if the genuine germ appears, your body is already prepared. Some vaccines require several doses or periodic boosters, because immune memory can fade over time. Vaccines also protect people indirectly. When a large enough share of a community is immune, a disease struggles to spread, which shields those who can't be vaccinated, such as newborn babies or people with weakened immune systems. This is known as herd immunity. It has been a decisive factor in the fight against many diseases, and in the case of smallpox, which once killed millions, vaccination ultimately led to its complete eradication in 1980.",
    qs: [
      { q: 'What is the talk mainly about?', o: ["How doctors choose a treatment", "Why some people become ill more often", "How vaccines prepare the immune system", "How viruses spread between countries"], a: 2, x: "Fonctionnement des vaccins et mémoire immunitaire." },
      { q: 'What are memory cells?', o: ["Cells that produce vaccines", "Cells that remember a germ and respond quickly to it", "Cells that cause illness", "Cells found only in the brain"], a: 1, x: "Cellules immunitaires qui gardent la mémoire d'un germe." },
      { q: 'What does the speaker imply about a vaccinated person who meets the real germ?', o: ["The response is faster, often before symptoms appear", "The person will certainly never be ill", "The person will need to take antibiotics", "The response is slower than in an unvaccinated person"], a: 0, x: "Les cellules mémoire répondent plus vite, souvent avant l'apparition de symptômes." },
      { q: 'Why might a person need a booster?', o: ["Vaccines are cheap to produce", "Immune memory can fade over time", "The first dose was too strong", "The germ becomes stronger every year"], a: 1, x: "« immune memory can fade over time »." },
      { q: 'Why does the speaker mention newborn babies and people with weakened immune systems?', o: ["They are the group most often vaccinated", "They cause diseases to spread", "They are the only ones who need boosters", "They depend on herd immunity because they cannot be vaccinated"], a: 3, x: "Ils ne peuvent pas être vaccinés et sont protégés par l'immunité collective." }] }
);
